<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Metsepole</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1206 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Livonian coastal district north of Riga, known from Henry of Livonia's chronicle and absorbed by the Archbishopric of Riga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Metsepole, whose name means 'forest land', was a district of the Finnic-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauja">Gauja</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbazi">Limbaži</a>. It appears in the chronicle of the Livonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in the early 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people lived from farming, fishing and coastal trade, led by local elders. Little else is recorded beyond their part in the wars and baptisms of the period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livs</a> of Metsepole submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert</a> and accepted baptism around 1206, after the failure of a Liv revolt. The area became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-riga">Archbishopric of Riga</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>, and the Livonian population merged gradually into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/metsepole.jpg" alt="Metsepole" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Garais · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metsepole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1206 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Finnic paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Livonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Metsapole, Liv land of Metsepole</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=metsepole" aria-label="Metsepole on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5210 230 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Metsepole lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5530 486L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5530 486h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5530 486h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1153&amp;focus=metsepole">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

